Speech Processor: s e opens the control by reading the current On/Off state and level, without changing anything. YAC changes only processor settings requested by your keys. It never changes RF power, frequency, mode, mic gain, equalizer or AMC and never keys transmission.

q means Finish and Keep. n means No changes: cancel and restore the processor state and level(s) saved on entry. If you have not edited anything, n sends no setters. After edits, cancellation restores exact native values and verifies readback. A failed or interrupted write still counts as a possible change. If restoration fails, YAC reports it and retains the entry snapshot during this run; reconnect/return to the original frequency, mode and receiver, release PTT and reopen s e to retry n. Original values are also recorded in the diagnostic log when logging is enabled. A program crash cannot guarantee restoration. q explicitly accepts current settings and discards the snapshot.

Release PTT before changing or restoring settings. e toggles On/Off. While On, full stop increases and comma decreases the selected strength. While Off, navigation leaves the level unchanged. Values stop at endpoints. v reads current values; h reads this help. Kenwood i selects Input and o selects Output; every write preserves the other live level. q and n return to Settings with Back.

Yaesu: one 0/1-100 strength setting, with one-unit steps. A zero read while Off may hide a retained level. YAC then refuses changes because it cannot guarantee exact cancellation; turn the processor On on the radio yourself and reopen s e if desired. Icom: a 0-10 strength scale encoded natively as 0-255; YAC uses native steps of 26 (roughly one strength unit), but saves the exact native value for cancellation. Kenwood: separate Input and Output, each 0-100 with one-unit steps. TS-480 identity must be verified. QMX/QMX Plus are not enabled. Yaesu/Icom voice processing is enabled here for LSB/USB; Kenwood also supports AM/FM. Consult your model's user guide; this help supplements it.

Meter feedback is optional: press Space, read the preparation prompt and press Space to acknowledge. Connect a correctly matched antenna for the test frequency or suitable dummy load; an unsuitable load can damage the transmitter. YAC cannot verify the load. Set appropriate power yourself. Lower power does not make an unsuitable load safe. Set basic mic gain first with processing off, using your radio's procedure. Speak normally, not loudly.

Once ready, hold PTT and speak normally; release PTT to hear the sampled peak COMP. Sampling may miss brief peaks. Readings are raw meter units, not strength settings or calibrated dB. Missing readings are unknown, not zero. On Kenwood select COMP for Input or ALC for Output on the radio if the read command does not return the required meter; Output selection reports sampled ALC rather than COMP. Follow the user guide for its ALC limit. The processor COMP and ALC tasks are different. Further Space preparation resets the peak. No automatic gain selection or in-range verdict is made.

Icom operating guidance generally uses 10-20dB compression in SSB. Kenwood input guidance uses about 10dB and output uses the ALC limit. Yaesu AMC guidance is separate from processor guidance; do not transfer targets automatically. Meter conversions vary by model and physical calibration is pending. User preparation and PTT remain in your hands.

